# 555 Timer LED Blinker on a Custom PCB

A 555 timer circuit in astable mode that blinks an LED at about 7 times per second. I designed the board in KiCad, etched it, soldered the components, and powered it with a 9V battery.


---

## Overview

- **Circuit:** 555 timer in astable mode
- **IC:** NA555P (DIP-8)
- **Supply:** 9V battery
- **Output:** LED blinking at about 7 Hz with about 52% duty cycle
- **Board:** Single-sided PCB with through-hole components
- **Design tool:** KiCad

---

## How It Works

In astable mode the 555 has no stable state. Its output (pin 3) keeps switching between HIGH and LOW without any external trigger.

1. The capacitor C2 charges through R1 and R2.
2. When its voltage reaches 2/3 of the supply, the output goes LOW and C2 starts discharging through R2.
3. When its voltage drops to 1/3 of the supply, the output goes HIGH and the cycle repeats.
4. The output drives the LED through the resistor R3, which limits the current.
5. C1 (0.01 µF) on pin 5 filters noise and keeps the thresholds stable.

---

## Calculations

Values used: R1 = 1 kΩ, R2 = 10 kΩ, C2 = 10 µF

- Frequency = 1.44 / ((R1 + 2 x R2) x C2) = 1.44 / (21k x 10µF) = about 6.9 Hz
- HIGH time = 0.693 x (R1 + R2) x C2 = about 76 ms
- LOW time = 0.693 x R2 x C2 = about 69 ms
- Duty cycle = (R1 + R2) / (R1 + 2 x R2) = about 52%
- LED current = (about 7.5V - about 2V) / 1k = about 5 mA

---

## Components

| Ref | Component | Value |
|---|---|---|
| U1 | 555 timer IC (NA555P) | DIP-8 |
| R1 | Resistor | 1 kΩ |
| R2 | Resistor | 10 kΩ |
| R3 | Resistor | 1 kΩ |
| C1 | Ceramic capacitor | 0.01 µF |
| C2 | Electrolytic capacitor | 10 µF |
| D1 | LED | 5 mm |
| J1 | 2-pin power connector | 2.54 mm |
| n/a | 9V battery with clip | 9V |

---

## PCB Design

- Designed in KiCad PCB Editor
- Single-sided layout with all routing on the bottom copper layer
- Wide 1.2 mm traces so the board is easy to etch by hand
- Ground connections routed around the edge of the board
- Design rule check completed with no unrouted nets

